# Let's Encrypt proves domain ownership by fetching /.well-known/acme-challenge/<token> over plain HTTP.
# The forced domain rules below would otherwise be free to swallow that request, and a certificate that
# never validates fails SILENTLY - the domain just keeps serving Netlify's *.netlify.app wildcard and the
# browser shows a privacy warning. So the challenge path is carved out first, and must stay first.
/.well-known/*    /.well-known/:splat    200

# Campaign short link on the forms domain. Points at the 60-Second Line Check micro-demo (Oct 2026),
# NOT the full Kitchen Line Walk - a cold ad click that lands on a 50-field form with two photo uploads
# and a signature converts at zero, which is what 276 clicks proved.
/try    /demo/    302

# kitchenchecklists.com is a domain alias on this project. All ad traffic for the buy lands here, so it
# goes to the micro-demo, which asks for one temperature before it asks for anything else.
# 302, not 301: browsers cache a 301 forever, which is how the old kitchen-line-walk target stuck.
#
# The full Kitchen Line Walk stays live and unchanged at /kitchen-line-walk/ for actual customers, and
# the "Try Our Inspection Forms" Zap still texts that URL directly with ?phone= appended - that chain
# does not route through this domain, so nothing below affects it.
http://kitchenchecklists.com/*    https://forms.inspectionreadykitchens.com/demo/    302!
https://kitchenchecklists.com/*   https://forms.inspectionreadykitchens.com/demo/    302!
